randomArrangement(ZZ,PolynomialRing,ZZ) -- generate an arrangement at random
****************************************************************************

  * Function: *note randomArrangement:
    randomArrangement_lpZZ_cmPolynomialRing_cmZZ_rp,
  * Usage: 
        randomArrangement(n,R,N)
  * Inputs:
      * n, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, number of hyperplanes
      * R, a *note polynomial ring: (Macaulay2Doc)PolynomialRing,, a polynomial
        ring over which to define the arrangement, or a number of variables l
        instead
      * N, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, absolute value of upper bound
        on coefficients
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Validate => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value false, if true, the method will attempt to return an arrangement
        whose underlying matroid is uniform.
  * Outputs:
      * a *note hyperplane arrangement: Arrangement,, a random rational
        arrangement of $n$ hyperplanes defined over $R$.

Description
===========

As $N$ increases, the random arrangement is a generic arrangement (i.e., a
realization of the *note uniform matroid: (Matroids)uniformMatroid, with
probability tending to 1.  The user can require that the arrangement generated
is actually generic by using the option Validate => true.

+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : randomArrangement(4,3,5)                                |
|                                                             |
|o1 = {- 4x  + 3x  + x , 3x  + x , - 2x  + 2x , 3x  + x  + x }|
|          1     2    3    1    3      1     3    1    2    3 |
|                                                             |
|o1 : Hyperplane Arrangement                                  |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+

If an arrangement has the *note poincare polynomial: poincare_lpArrangement_rp,
of a generic arrangement, then it is itself generic.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : tally apply(12, i -> poincare randomArrangement(6,3,5))                 |
|                                                                             |
|                       2      3                                              |
|o2 = Tally{1 + 6T + 15T  + 10T  => 8}                                        |
|                       2     3                                               |
|           1 + 6T + 14T  + 9T  => 4                                          |
|                                                                             |
|o2 : Tally                                                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : A = randomArrangement(6,3,5,Validate=>true)                             |
|                                                                             |
|o3 = {5x  + 4x  + 3x , - 3x  + x  - 5x , - 5x  - 3x  + 3x , 3x  + 4x  - 2x , |
|        1     2     3      1    2     3      1     2     3    1     2     3  |
|                                                                             |
|o3 : Hyperplane Arrangement                                                  |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|- 2x  - 3x  + 5x , 5x  + 3x  - 5x }                                          |
|    1     2     3    1     2     3                                           |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : U = uniformMatroid(3,6);                                                |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : assert areIsomorphic(U, matroid A)                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

If the user specifies Validate => true and $N$ is too small, the method may not
halt.

See also
========

  * *note genericArrangement: genericArrangement_lpZZ_cmZZ_cmRing_rp, --
    realize the uniform matroid using points on the monomial curve

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note randomArrangement(ZZ,PolynomialRing,ZZ):
    randomArrangement_lpZZ_cmPolynomialRing_cmZZ_rp, -- generate an arrangement
    at random
  * "randomArrangement(ZZ,ZZ,ZZ)"
